From 066e80e3aa484d720e4c1b1f3566007f4eefae1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gtguerreiro-max <295622337+gtguerreiro-max@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:52:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 58/60] kernel: guest delays and yields stay in the process
 under Wine

A guest delay (KeDelayExecutionThread, a game's Sleep) armed a high
resolution waitable timer and waited on it alertably, two wineserver
round trips under Wine, then spun its last 250 us; an alertable yield
(Sleep(0)) was SleepEx(0, TRUE), a wineserver round trip too. A game's
Sleep(1) loop took a quarter of a core on the Steam Deck, and Forza
Horizon's render thread yields about 12,000 times a second.

With runtime.fast_sleeps (on) a delay sleeps on the thread's park word,
patch 0057's, with rex::platform::SleepOnWord: RtlWaitOnAddress with a
100 ns timeout under Wine, a futex in the process (elsewhere it declines
and the timer stays: Windows rounds such a sleep up to its timer period).
Stop, suspend and APC requests already set the park word's interrupt and
wake it (InterruptFastWait); the request's host callback then runs, and
an alertable delay returns STATUS_USER_APC. An alertable yield looks at
the park word and calls SwitchToThread (sched_yield there).
REX_PLATFORM_HAS_SLEEP_ON_WORD tells code built against several SDKs.

Steam Deck, fast_sleeps false -> true, two runs each: Iron Brigade at
60 fps, its Sleep(1) thread 24.2/24.5 -> 8.5/8.4 % of a core, main
thread 52.9/51.7 -> 44.9/42.1 %; Forza Horizon at 30 fps, wineserver
12/12 -> 7/6 %, its Sleep(1) thread 36.6/36.7 -> 21.5/20.8 %.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
